                <section class="content-section" id="remediation">
                    <h2>Remediation Options for Non-Compliance</h2>
                    <p>
                        When Reconciliation results in a non-compliant status (you are using more than you own), the system generates actionable <strong>Remediation Options</strong>:
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Purchase Rights:</strong> Automatically generates a Purchase Order request to buy the shortfall amount of licenses.</li>
                        <li><strong>Create Allocations:</strong> If you have unused pool licenses, you can assign them to the rogue installations to make them compliant.</li>
                        <li><strong>Remove Unallocated:</strong> Trigger a workflow to immediately uninstall the software from machines that shouldn't have it.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <section class="content-section" id="reclamation">
                    <h2>Software Reclamation Rules</h2>
                    <p>
                        Reclamation is how organizations save millions of dollars. It focuses on harvesting unused software. 
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        A <strong>Reclamation Rule</strong> (<code>samp_sw_reclamation_rule</code>) defines conditions. For example: <em>"If Microsoft Visio has not been launched in 90 days, target it for removal."</em>
                    </p>
                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">The Reclamation Workflow</div>
                        <div class="mermaid">
                        sequenceDiagram
                            participant SAM as SAM Engine
                            participant User as Employee
                            participant Client as SCCM / Jamf
                            SAM->>SAM: Identifies usage < 90 days
                            SAM->>User: Sends email: "Do you still need Visio?"
                            User-->>SAM: Ignores or replies "No"
                            SAM->>Client: Triggers uninstall workflow
                            Client-->>SAM: Confirms software removed
                            SAM->>SAM: License returned to pool
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        By integrating with SCCM, Jamf, or Intune (via Flow Designer or Service Graph Connectors), this entire process can be fully automated—zero touch from IT.
                    </p>
                </section>
        
